description:
Take URLs apart and handle their encoding (lesson 3.03):

```cpp
struct Url { std::string scheme, host; int port; std::string path, query, fragment; };

std::optional<Url> parseUrl(const std::string& text);
std::string percentEncode(const std::string& text);
std::optional<std::string> percentDecode(const std::string& text);
std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>> parseQuery(const std::string& query);
```

- **parseUrl** handles `scheme://host[:port][/path][?query][#fragment]`. The scheme (letters, digits, `+`, `-`, `.`, starting with a letter) is returned in lower case; the host in lower case, with an IPv6 host written in brackets returned without them. The port defaults to 80 for `http` and 443 for `https` (0 for any other scheme when absent); a port must be digits from 1 to 65535. The path is `/` when absent. Query and fragment exclude their `?` and `#`. The parts stay percent-encoded. A missing `://`, an empty host, or a bad port gives `std::nullopt`.
- **percentEncode** leaves letters, digits, `-`, `.`, `_` and `~` as they are and writes every other byte as `%` and two **uppercase** hex digits.
- **percentDecode** turns `%XY` (either case) back into the byte; a `%` not followed by two hex digits gives `std::nullopt`.
- **parseQuery** splits at `&` into `name=value` pairs (a pair without `=` has an empty value; empty pieces are skipped), turns `+` into a space, then percent-decodes both sides; a pair that fails to decode is skipped.

